def footer(canvas, doc):
    canvas.saveState(); w,h=A4
    canvas.setStrokeColor(colors.HexColor('#CBD5E1')); canvas.line(25*mm,17*mm,w-25*mm,17*mm)
    canvas.setFont('DV',7); canvas.setFillColor(colors.HexColor('#64748B'))
    canvas.drawString(25*mm,12*mm,'Assignment 1 | Sudoku SAT')
    canvas.drawRightString(w-25*mm,12*mm,str(doc.page))
    canvas.restoreState()

doc=SimpleDocTemplate(str(OUT),pagesize=A4,rightMargin=25*mm,leftMargin=25*mm,topMargin=22*mm,bottomMargin=23*mm,title='SAT Encodings for Sudoku: Implementation and Benchmark Study',author='[ADD AUTHOR NAME]')
story=[]
story += [Spacer(1,14*mm),P('SAT Encodings for Sudoku:<br/>Implementation and Benchmark Study','TitleCustom'),P('Assignment 1 — Modern Problems in Computer Science','SubTitle'),Spacer(1,8*mm),P('<b>Author:</b> [ADD AUTHOR NAME] &nbsp;&nbsp; <b>Student ID:</b> [ADD STUDENT ID]','SubTitle'),P('<b>Affiliation:</b> [ADD AFFILIATION]','SubTitle'),Spacer(1,10*mm),HRFlowable(width='100%',color=colors.HexColor('#CBD5E1')),Spacer(1,5*mm)]
story += [P('Abstract','H1x'),P('This report describes a Sudoku solver that constructs a conjunctive normal form (CNF) formula and solves it with SAT solvers exposed by PySAT. The implementation supports eight at-most-one (AMO) encodings, optional redundant at-least-one (ALO) constraints for units, solution decoding, and independent validation. The committed benchmark snapshot contains 527 rows: SAT and baseline solving on 9×9 through 36×36 grids, and encoding-only measurements through 64×64. The tracked instances use three generated puzzles per size at a 0.4 clue keep ratio and do not check uniqueness. We report descriptive counts and formula sizes, distinguish solve outcomes from encoding-only rows, and avoid ranking encodings from this small, unpartitioned legacy sample. Newer local schema-v3 results are untracked and are not used here.'),P('<b>Keywords:</b> Sudoku; SAT; CNF; PySAT; cardinality constraints; benchmark reproducibility','Smallx'),P('1. Introduction','H1x'),P('Sudoku is a finite constraint satisfaction problem. For a grid of side length N=b², each cell receives one of N symbols, and every row, column, and b×b box contains each symbol exactly once. This project studies a propositional encoding of those constraints and compares SAT solving with exact CP and MIP baselines where the local environment permitted runs.'),P('Assignment 1 asks students to implement a Sudoku or N-Queens solver using SAT solvers through PySAT, conduct experiments including large matrices, compare SAT with exact alternatives including CPLEX CP, CPLEX MIP, Gurobi, and CP-SAT, and present the work as a scientific-style paper with a source/results repository link in Data Availability. It encourages AMO/ALO encodings such as binary, commander, product, and sequential counter. The accompanying sample is used only for paper structure; its 2DBMP topic and claims do not apply to Sudoku. The assignment lists an Elsevier CAS LaTeX class as an example. That class file was not present in the repository or local TeX installation; this report uses a portable article layout pending confirmation whether CAS is mandatory.'),P('2. Sudoku model and CNF encoding','H1x'),P('2.1 Variables and constraints','H2x'),P('For each row r, column c, and value v in {1,…,N}, the encoder defines Boolean variable x(r,c,v), true when cell (r,c) contains v. Primary variable identifiers occupy 1 through N³; auxiliary variables used by cardinality encodings follow them. Each cell has an exactly-one constraint over its N value variables. For each value, each row, column, and box has an at-most-one constraint. Given clues become unit clauses. The implementation can additionally emit at-least-one clauses for row/value, column/value, and box/value groups; these are controlled by unit_alo and are redundant given cell exactly-one and unit AMO clauses. Cell ALO clauses remain required.'),P('2.2 AMO and ALO encodings','H2x'),P('The repository implements pairwise, sequential, commander, binary, and product AMO encodings in src/encodings/amo.py. The commander family has published CNF applications [2]. It also exposes PySAT-generated pysat_seqcounter, pysat_bitwise, and pysat_ladder through the PySAT toolkit [1]. ALO is a single positive clause containing the group variables (src/encodings/alo.py). Tests check AMO behavior exhaustively over selected group sizes: all assignments with at most one true input remain satisfiable, while assignments with any pair of true inputs are rejected. This is implementation-level evidence, not a formal proof for every possible auxiliary assignment.'),P('2.3 Solving, decoding, and validation','H2x'),P('The materialized solver path builds CNF and passes it to a PySAT solver. A streaming path can generate clauses directly into a solver while preserving encoding semantics; it was not used for committed schema-v1 result rows. A satisfying assignment is decoded using primary cell variables. Decoded grids are validated against dimensions, value ranges, original clues, and all row, column, and box constraints before a benchmark row is marked valid. Optional uniqueness checking blocks the selected cell assignment and solves again; UNSAT on the blocked formula indicates uniqueness. The committed benchmark did not enable this check.'),P('3. Implementation and experimental setup','H1x'),P('3.1 Solvers and benchmark snapshot','H2x'),P('SAT benchmark rows use PySAT identifiers cadical195, glucose42, kissat404, and minisat22. Requested exact baselines are CP-SAT, Gurobi, CPLEX MIP, and CPLEX CP; CBC ILP is an additional baseline. In the committed data, some Gurobi and CPLEX outcomes are recorded as license_limit. These are reported as recorded outcomes, not inferred timeouts or solver failures. Whether a different academic/commercial license changes each limit requires a run with that license.'),P('This report uses results/results.csv, the tracked schema-v1 file, with 527 rows and 17 columns. The canonical run used a 60-second requested timeout, three puzzles per box size, clue keep ratio 0.4, and solving sizes 9, 16, 25, and 36. Puzzle seeds followed the legacy rule 1000b+i (b is box size, i is puzzle index). Sizes 49 and 64 are encoding-only. Pairwise encoding was skipped at 64 due to memory constraints. Uniqueness was not checked; generated puzzles inherit at least one completion but are not guaranteed unique.'),P('Metadata records Python 3.14.4 for this run and source commit 0dcf16efbbcd005328ce590fe2611af262343f23; CI targets Python 3.12. Current benchmark tooling describes richer schema-v4 provenance, but those guarantees must not be attributed retroactively to v1 rows. A separate 3,753-row schema-v3 file exists only as an untracked local artifact and is excluded below.'),P('Recorded outcome counts','H2x')]
data=[[P('<b>Outcome</b>','Tablex'),P('<b>Count</b>','Tablex'),P('<b>Interpretation</b>','Tablex')],['Solved','444','SAT and baseline rows'],['Timeout','13','Recorded timeout status'],['License limit','23','Recorded baseline status'],['Encoded','47','Formula-size experiment; no solving'],['Total','527','All tracked CSV rows']]
t=Table(data,colWidths=[42*mm,22*mm,85*mm],repeatRows=1)
t.setStyle(TableStyle([('BACKGROUND',(0,0),(-1,0),colors.HexColor('#DCE6F1')),('GRID',(0,0),(-1,-1),0.4,colors.HexColor('#94A3B8')),('FONTNAME',(0,0),(-1,-1),'DV'),('FONTSIZE',(0,0),(-1,-1),8),('VALIGN',(0,0),(-1,-1),'TOP'),('ROWBACKGROUNDS',(0,1),(-1,-1),[colors.white,colors.HexColor('#F8FAFC')]),('LEFTPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),6),('RIGHTPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),6),('TOPPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),5),('BOTTOMPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),5)])); story += [t,Spacer(1,5),P('The 47 encode-only rows cover 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, and 64; solving rows occur only at 9 through 36. Among 60 baseline rows: CP-SAT solved 12; CBC solved 7 and timed out 5; CPLEX CP solved 7 and recorded 5 license limits; CPLEX MIP solved 3 and recorded 9 license limits; Gurobi solved 3 and recorded 9 license limits. These counts describe this sample and environment only.')]
story += [P('4. Results','H1x'),P('4.1 Solving outcomes and limits','H2x'),P('The schema-v1 audit reports 444 solved rows and confirms all 444 have valid=True. The audit also re-verifies 132 saved/generated puzzle records against seeds and hashes. This supports the recorded instances and validator path, but does not establish uniqueness, generalize to untested puzzles, or replace independent replication.'),P('Figure 1 visualizes available baseline results from the canonical CSV. Only three puzzles per size and one clue density were used, with legacy unpartitioned configuration selection, timeout rows, and license-limited cells. It is descriptive; no method is declared fastest.'),Image(str(ROOT/'report/figures/fig_vs_baselines.png'),width=158*mm,height=87*mm),P('Figure 1. Existing baseline comparison generated from tracked schema-v1 data. Timeouts and license limits remain distinct.','Captionx'),P('4.2 Encoding size','H2x'),P('The encoding-size figure and table describe formula variables and clauses. At N=36, pairwise has 3,271,602 clauses and 46,656 variables, while product has 534,450 clauses and 108,864 variables. At N=64, product has 2,836,149 clauses and 720,896 variables. These are formula-size measurements only: fewer clauses or variables do not imply shorter solving time or lower peak memory. No size experiment at 49 or 64 solved the formula.'),Image(str(ROOT/'report/figures/fig_encoding_size.png'),width=152*mm,height=82*mm),P('Figure 2. Existing encoding-size figure from the canonical result set; encode-only rows are not solving results.','Captionx')]
